---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import RuntimeLayout from "../../components/RuntimeLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const cfg = `import watershed/crdt_js

// Auto starts on WebRTC and attempts the relay in parallel — it never
// waits for one, and prefers it while healthy.
let config =
  crdt_js.config(
    room_id: "retro-board-9c2",
    replica_label: "ada",
    compatibility_tag: "retro-board/v1",
    root: crdt_js.pn_counter(),
    signaling: signaling,
  )
  |> crdt_js.with_transport_policy(crdt_js.Auto)
  |> crdt_js.with_sequencer(crdt_js.sequencer("wss://relay.example/room"))`;
---

<RuntimeLayout
  slug="p2p"
  description="How a watershed CRDT document runs on a WebRTC mesh with no sequencer joined at all: signaling only introduces peers, eligible structures converge by merge instead of server order, and an optional relay attaches later for durability without changing the document's state or public handles."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>A document that doesn't need a sequencer</h2>
    <p>
      Most watershed documents use server order to resolve concurrent writes.
      A <strong>CRDT document</strong> does not require this order. Browsers find
      each other through WebRTC, edit while disconnected, and converge after
      they exchange state. Each eligible structure defines its own merge rule.
      The <a href={`/examples#clap_counter_lustre`}>clap counter example</a>
      runs without a sequencer. Two tabs can add claps at the same time without
      acknowledgments.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Signaling introduces peers; it never sequences</h2>
    <p>
      A signaling service exchanges offers, answers, and ICE candidates so that
      two browsers can connect directly. It can also authenticate room access.
      It does not order operations or store document state. After the data
      channel opens, signaling receives only opaque envelopes. A signaling
      outage prevents new peer discovery but does not affect connected peers.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first topology is a small full mesh: every peer opens one data
      channel to every other peer, capped at eight, and the ninth arrival is
      refused with <code>RoomFull</code> rather than degrading the mesh
      underneath everyone already in it. Applications supply their own STUN
      and TURN servers (watershed ships neither); host candidates alone only
      connect peers with no NAT between them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Not every structure is eligible</h2>
    <p>
      Only CRDT channels can run without server order:
      <a href="/structures/counters#pn">PN counter</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sets#orset">OR-set</a>,
      the <a href="/structures/maps#ormap">OR-map</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sets#gset">G-set</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sets#twopset">2P-set</a>,
      <a href="/structures/sequences#sequence">SharedSequence</a>, and
      <a href="/structures/sequences#text">SharedText</a>. A p2p document's
      root is one of these, chosen explicitly at <code>config</code> time,
      never the ordinary last-write-wins <a href="/structures/maps">SharedMap</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything that depends on server order or acknowledgement stays out:
      <a href="/structures/maps">SharedMap</a>,
      <a href="/structures/counters">SharedCounter</a>, and
      <a href="/structures/maps#directory">SharedDirectory</a> resolve by
      sequence number; <a href="/structures/coordination#claims">claims</a>
      and <a href="/structures/coordination#registers">registers</a> persist
      one; <a href="/structures/coordination#tasks">TaskManager</a>,
      <a href="/structures/coordination#pact">pact maps</a>, and{" "}
      <a href="/structures/coordination#ordered">ordered collections</a>{" "}
      depend on ordered membership; and the OT-transformed{" "}
      <a href="/structures/transforms">json_ot and rich-text</a> channels
      transform against sequenced history. None of these have a merge
      function to fall back on, so none of them are safe with no sequencer in
      the room.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>One document, three policies</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>CrdtDocument</code> chooses its transports once, at
      <code>config</code> time:
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={cfg} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      <strong><code>Auto</code></strong> is the default: the mesh and any
      configured relay each come up on their own schedule, readiness waits
      only for the mesh, and the relay becomes the durable delta path once it
      proves itself. <strong><code>P2pOnly</code></strong>{" "}
      opens no relay socket and ignores a configured one entirely.
      <strong><code>SequencedOnly</code></strong> is the inverse: no
      signaling, no <code>RTCPeerConnection</code>, and readiness waits on the
      relay alone, under a bounded deadline, because there is nothing else to
      wait on. All three run the identical document over the identical wire
      envelopes, so a snapshot exported under one policy imports under
      either of the others.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Losing the relay doesn't lose the document.</strong> When a
        primary relay connection drops, the document reports the failover,
        pushes the digest it owed the mesh, asks its peers for state, and
        keeps running on WebRTC: same public handles, same converged state,
        one transport quietly retired. Readiness under
        <code>Auto</code> never waited on the relay in the first place.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>An optional durable relay</h2>
    <p>
      A relay that uses <code>crdt_relay_v1</code> provides durable storage and
      fan-out. It records a diagnostic order, broadcasts accepted data, and
      returns stored state on request. It does not merge data, decode kernel
      payloads, or select a winning replica. Connected replicas perform the
      merge. Without a relay, connected replicas hold the room state.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">In short</span>
    <p>
      A CRDT document trades the server's order for a merge rule, so it can
      run on a WebRTC mesh with no sequencer at all. Signaling only
      introduces peers; eligible structures converge by merge; and an
      optional relay attaches later purely for durability and reach, never
      changing what the document means.
    </p>
  </div>
</RuntimeLayout>
